            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "Uptime, form, and key-page testing.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 4,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "Performance checks for important landing pages.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 5,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "Database cleanup and removal of unused components.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 6,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "Recovery steps when an update causes a conflict.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 7,
        },
      ],
      "listType": "bullet",
      "tag": "ul",
      "type": "list",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Updates matter because WordPress sites are a patchwork of software written by different teams. So, even if you only change one plugin, you can still break a layout, a script, a form integration, or a payment flow.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "The safe workflow is rarely “click update all and hope.” It looks more like: backup first, update in a controlled way, verify the money paths, and keep a rollback option ready.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Security is the other big cost driver. WordPress powers approximately 43% of all websites, and plugins account for approximately 97% of WordPress vulnerabilities, according to compiled 2026 maintenance statistics (",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "WordPress maintenance statistics",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "title": null,
          "type": "link",
          "url": "https://webfixit.io/wordpress-maintenance-statistics",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "). Those numbers don’t mean every site needs an expensive enterprise package. But they do mean “we’ve never been hacked” isn’t a plan — it’s just a lucky streak.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Why provider quotes differ",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h3",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "When you compare pricing, you’re not just comparing time. You’re comparing what the provider agrees to be responsible for.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "A freelancer might charge for scheduled work and then bill extra hours when things go sideways. An agency might bundle monitoring, support, reporting, and emergency response. Another provider might automate updates but exclude content edits and “weird issues,” which is often where time goes.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "So, when you’re staring at two quotes and wondering why they’re far apart, ask questions that force clarity:",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "After an update breaks a lead form, who fixes it — and is that included?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 1,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "Do they test changes on staging, or only on live?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 2,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "How fast do they respond when the site is down?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 3,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "Is malware removal included, or treated as a separate project?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 4,
        },
      ],
      "listType": "bullet",
      "tag": "ul",
      "type": "list",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Also, separate recurring care from one-time repairs. If a provider offers one flat monthly fee, request an itemized scope anyway. It’s not nitpicking; it’s how you avoid discovering exclusions during an outage.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "A quick way to map cost to “coverage”",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h3",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "I like to think of wordpress maintenance cost as buying coverage levels.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Coverage level 1: automation only.",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " You get updates/backups scheduled. If something breaks, you’re still the coordinator.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 1,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Coverage level 2: automation + human checks.",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " Updates happen with basic validation (forms/pages) and someone will roll back if needed.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 2,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Coverage level 3: managed ownership.",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " Monitoring, security follow-up, troubleshooting, and clearer responsibility when the site acts up.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 3,
        },
      ],
      "listType": "bullet",
      "tag": "ul",
      "type": "list",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "The right level depends on how allergic your business is to downtime and how technical your internal team is.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "A common “cheap plan” failure mode (hypothetical, but typical)",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h3",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Here’s the failure pattern that makes people hate maintenance spending.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "You buy a low-cost plan that runs updates. One update changes something subtle — maybe a form plugin or a caching setup. The website still loads, so nobody panics. Meanwhile, the contact form stops delivering, or tracking stops recording conversions.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Two weeks later, you notice leads are down. Now you’re paying for troubleshooting under pressure, which tends to be slower and more expensive. You didn’t just pay money; you also lost time and data.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "That’s why I’d rather see a slightly higher monthly spend that includes basic testing than a rock-bottom fee that only covers the click-work.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "WordPress Maintenance Services and Plan Choices",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h2",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "WordPress maintenance services usually land in three models: self-maintained, assisted, and fully managed. The right model depends on your time, your technical confidence, and what a failed website costs you.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Self-maintained WordPress sites",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h3",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Self-maintenance can be totally fine for a simple site — if someone truly owns it.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "That owner needs administrator access, a routine, backup checks, update notes, and a recovery plan. “I’ll get to it later” doesn’t survive a busy month. Calendar ownership does.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "The hidden cost is focus. One 2026 source estimates DIY maintenance can take five or more hours monthly, while professional maintenance ranges from $39 to $359 or more per month (",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "WordPress maintenance statistics",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "title": null,
          "type": "link",
          "url": "https://webfixit.io/wordpress-maintenance-statistics",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "). Those hours may be reasonable for a webmaster. But if you’re the business owner, five hours can be sales calls, hiring, or client delivery — and you’ll feel that tradeoff.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "A self-maintenance routine that doesn’t fall apart",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h4",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "If you’re going to keep wordpress maintenance cost low by doing it yourself, make it boring and repeatable:",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "1) ",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Take a backup before any updates.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "2) ",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Update in small batches.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "   If you update everything at once and something breaks, you won’t know which change caused it.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "3) ",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Test the money paths.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "   Submit the main form, run a test purchase if you sell, test login if you have members.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "4) ",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Check basics.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "   Scan key pages on mobile, confirm navigation and headers aren’t broken.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "5) ",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Write a tiny changelog.",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "type": "linebreak",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "   A couple lines: what updated, when, and what you tested. Later you’ll thank yourself.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "The mistake is thinking self-maintenance is “free.” It’s not. You pay with attention, and attention is expensive.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Assisted and managed plans",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h3",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "An assisted plan usually covers recurring updates and backups, with support available if something fails. A managed plan often goes further: staging tests, security monitoring, performance checks, content edits, and emergency recovery.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "That said, the label is less important than the scope. Some “managed” offers are basically automated updates with a friendly name. Others include real human review and responsibility. So, read the actual service list and ask what happens during incidents.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Research published by WP Engine says 80% of web design professionals now offer maintenance packages (",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "maintenance package research",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "title": null,
          "type": "link",
          "url": "https://wpengine.com/blog/wordpress-maintenance-plans",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "). That’s good news for buyers because you have options. But it also means you need to compare offers on outcomes, not marketing language.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "How I’d compare two plans (apples-to-apples)",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h4",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "When you’re comparing providers, I’d push for answers to a few specific questions:",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Update process:",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " Do they update on a schedule? Do they use staging? Do they roll back if needed?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 1,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Testing:",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " Do they test forms, checkout, and key landing pages after updates?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 2,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Backups:",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " Where are backups stored, and will they actually restore one if asked?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 3,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Security:",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " Are they only “monitoring,” or will they handle cleanup and follow-up?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 4,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Support:",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": " What counts as included support versus billable work?",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 5,
        },
      ],
      "listType": "bullet",
      "tag": "ul",
      "type": "list",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "You’re trying to find out whether you’re buying automation, expertise, or accountability.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "A useful plan comparison",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h3",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Self-managed:",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": " lowest cash spend, highest owner responsibility, and slower recovery unless skills are available.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Automated care:",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": " predictable recurring cost, but limited judgment when plugins conflict.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 1,
          "text": "Managed WordPress maintenance plans:",
          "type": "text",
        },
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": " higher monthly spend, yet clearer accountability and faster support.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "My advice is blunt: don’t buy the longest feature list. Buy protection for the customer path that pays you.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "For a local service company, that’s usually the call-to-action and contact form. For a marketer, it’s campaign pages and tracking integrity. For a webmaster, it’s safe deployments and quick rollback.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "A practical “pick the model” example",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h4",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "If your wordpress maintenance cost budget is tight, decide where you’ll accept risk.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "If the site is mostly informational and rarely changes, self-managed or basic assisted care might be fine.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 1,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "If you run paid campaigns or rely on form submissions daily, you’ll want at least assisted care with testing.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 2,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "If you sell products, run memberships, or depend on bookings, managed support is usually the calmer choice because incidents aren’t theoretical — they’re inevitable.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 3,
        },
      ],
      "listType": "bullet",
      "tag": "ul",
      "type": "list",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "Common plan-choice mistakes (and what I’d do instead)",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "tag": "h4",
      "type": "heading",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Mistake: choosing based on the lowest monthly price.",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "  Instead, choose based on “what happens when checkout breaks?” or “who fixes the form?”",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 1,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Mistake: assuming hosting support equals maintenance.",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "  Hosting support can be helpful, but it often won’t cover plugin conflicts, theme issues, or content-related breakage.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 2,
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "format": 1,
              "text": "Mistake: buying managed support but still acting like you’re on your own.",
              "type": "text",
            },
            {
              "type": "linebreak",
            },
            {
              "format": 0,
              "text": "  If you pay for a plan, actually use it: report weird behavior early, ask for clarity, and keep your key workflows documented.",
              "type": "text",
            },
          ],
          "type": "listitem",
          "value": 3,
        },
      ],
      "listType": "bullet",
      "tag": "ul",
      "type": "list",
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "format": 0,
          "text": "If you want a clean next step, map your money paths, list your break points, then pick the smallest plan that still covers updates, backups, testing, and recovery. That’s how you keep wordpress maintenance cost predictable — and your site dependable.",
          "type": "text",
        },
      ],
      "type": "paragraph",
    },
  ],
  "type": "root",
}
`;
